CHOWN(2)                       P32W Unix                        CHOWN(2)

NAME
       chown - change the owner and group of a file

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/stat.h>

       int chown(const char *path, int uid, int gid);

DESCRIPTION
       chown makes uid the owner and gid the group of the file
       path. Only the super-user may call it. The file's change
       time is set to now.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EPERM   The caller is not the super-user.
       ENOENT  path does not exist.
       ENOTDIR A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
       EACCES  Search permission is denied on a directory in the
               path.
       EFAULT  path is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               path is 256 characters or more, or a component is
               longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       chmod(2), stat(2), chown(1), passwd(5), group(5)

BUGS
       Both IDs are always set: -1 does not mean "leave
       unchanged". The set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits are not
       cleared. The prototype is in <sys/stat.h> rather than
       <unistd.h>.
